# SYNTHETIC FIXTURE — ncp-config-kmp.bin

This is a synthetic binary oNCP/KMP message built for the offline tests. It is
not a captured gateway response. The addresses `10.20.0.x` are documentation
placeholders, not a real network: internal IP `10.20.0.10/32`, gateway
`10.20.0.1`, split routes `10.0.0.0/8`, `172.30.0.0/21`, `172.30.8.0/24`,
`172.30.6.0/24`, DNS `10.20.0.53` and `10.20.0.54`, MTU 1396, ESP AES-128/SHA1
on UDP 4500, ESP->SSL fallback 15 s.

Wire values follow the oNCP reference layout, not a guess: ESP encryption
0x02 = AES-128-CBC (0x05 would be AES-256-CBC), ESP authentication
0x02 = HMAC-SHA1 (0x01 would be HMAC-MD5).

The ESP key block and outbound SPI are synthetic values, included so the tests
can prove key material is never rendered. The first TLV group (0xFFFF/1)
carries this note as an unknown attribute, which also exercises
unknown-attribute tolerance.

The binary exchange happens in the oNCP handshake after the HTTP login, not in
the login response body, so this blob only mirrors that shape.
